Here's the full program:

```csharp
using System.Text.Json;

var person = new Person("Alice", 30);

var options = new JsonSerializerOptions
{
    PropertyNamingPolicy = JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase,
    WriteIndented = true
};

Console.WriteLine(JsonSerializer.Serialize(person, options));

public record Person(string name, int age);
```

**Output:**
```json
{
  "Name": "Alice",
  "Age": 30
}
```

`JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase` is a built-in policy available in .NET 11. C# positional records already generate PascalCase properties (`Name`, `Age`) from the lowercase parameters, so the default policy would also work here. But using `PascalCase` explicitly is useful if you ever mix in properties with other casing conventions — it will normalize everything to PascalCase.
